A `Grid` object stores a rectangular array of integers in a two-dimensional array instance variable named `cells`. The array has at least one row and at least one column.

```java
public class Grid {
    private int[][] cells;

    /** Returns the sum of the values in row r of cells. Precondition: 0 <= r < cells.length */
    public int rowSum(int r)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns the index of the row of cells with the greatest row sum. If more than one row
     *  has the greatest sum, returns the smallest such index.
     *  Precondition: cells has at least one row */
    public int largestRow()
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }

    /** Returns a new array in which element c is the sum of the values in column c of cells. */
    public int[] columnSums()
    { /* to be implemented in part (c) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `rowSum`.

**(b)** Write the method `largestRow`, using `rowSum` as appropriate.

**(c)** Write the method `columnSums`. For example, if `cells` is {{4, 1, 7}, {2, 9, 0}, {5, 5, 5}, {1, 1, 1}}, then `columnSums` returns {12, 16, 13}.

sampleResponse:
```java
public int rowSum(int r) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int value : cells[r]) {
        sum += value;
    }
    return sum;
}

public int largestRow() {
    int best = 0;
    for (int r = 1; r < cells.length; r++) {
        if (rowSum(r) > rowSum(best)) {
            best = r;
        }
    }
    return best;
}

public int[] columnSums() {
    int[] sums = new int[cells[0].length];
    for (int r = 0; r < cells.length; r++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < cells[r].length; c++) {
            sums[c] += cells[r][c];
        }
    }
    return sums;
}
```
In `largestRow`, the strict `>` means that a later row with an equal sum does not replace the earlier one, which gives the smallest index for ties.

prompt:
A `Board` object stores the contents of a rectangular game board in a two-dimensional `int` array named `spots`. Each element is 0 for an empty spot or the number of the player (1 or 2) who occupies it.

```java
public class Board {
    private int[][] spots;

    /** Returns true if every spot in at least one row of spots is occupied by player;
     *  otherwise returns false. */
    public boolean hasFullRow(int player)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns the number of spots adjacent to the spot at (row, col), including diagonal
     *  neighbors, that are occupied by player. The spot (row, col) itself is not counted.
     *  Spots outside the board are not counted.
     *  Precondition: (row, col) is a valid position in spots. */
    public int countNeighbors(int row, int col, int player)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `hasFullRow`.

**(b)** Write the method `countNeighbors`. For example, if `spots` is {{1, 2, 1}, {1, 1, 1}, {2, 1, 0}}, then `countNeighbors(1, 1, 1)` returns 5 and `countNeighbors(0, 0, 1)` returns 2.

sampleResponse:
```java
public boolean hasFullRow(int player) {
    for (int r = 0; r < spots.length; r++) {
        boolean full = true;
        for (int c = 0; c < spots[r].length; c++) {
            if (spots[r][c] != player) {
                full = false;
            }
        }
        if (full) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

public int countNeighbors(int row, int col, int player) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int r = row - 1; r <= row + 1; r++) {
        for (int c = col - 1; c <= col + 1; c++) {
            if (r >= 0 && r < spots.length && c >= 0 && c < spots[0].length
                    && !(r == row && c == col) && spots[r][c] == player) {
                count++;
            }
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
The bounds tests come first in the condition, so `spots[r][c]` is evaluated only for valid positions (short-circuit evaluation).
